    "coverImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/92/TawangMonastery.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled",
    "description": "Perched at 10,000 feet near the Tibetan border, Tawang is the birthplace of the 6th Dalai Lama and home to India’s largest and most majestic Buddhist monastery.",
    "longDescription": "In the 17th century, Merak Lama Lodre Gyatso sought a celestial site to construct a sanctuary. Unable to choose, he prayed inside a cave. When he emerged, his white horse had vanished. Following the tracks up the mountain, he found the horse standing peacefully on a breathtaking cliff edge bathed in a beam of golden sunlight. He named it Tawang (Ta meaning horse, Wang meaning chosen by divine will).\n\nFounded in 1680–81 CE, Galden Namgey Lhatse (Tawang Monastery) houses over 450 lamas, a 28-foot gilded Buddha statue, and priceless manuscripts written in gold ink. Guarding the pass below is the Sela Pass at 13,700 feet, forever linked with the bravery of Rifleman Jaswant Singh Rawat in the 1962 conflict.\n\nTawang invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Arunachal Pradesh.",
    "loreTitle": "The Vision of Merak Lama & The Birth of Tawang Gompa",
    "ancientLegend": "In the 17th century, Merak Lama Lodre Gyatso sought a celestial site to construct a sanctuary. Unable to choose, he prayed inside a cave. When he emerged, his white horse had vanished. Following the tracks up the mountain, he found the horse standing peacefully on a breathtaking cliff edge bathed in a beam of golden sunlight. He named it Tawang (Ta meaning horse, Wang meaning chosen by divine will).",
    "historicalChronicle": "Founded in 1680–81 CE, Galden Namgey Lhatse (Tawang Monastery) houses over 450 lamas, a 28-foot gilded Buddha statue, and priceless manuscripts written in gold ink. Guarding the pass below is the Sela Pass at 13,700 feet, forever linked with the bravery of Rifleman Jaswant Singh Rawat in the 1962 conflict.",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The Spirit Guard of Jaswant Garh: Soldiers stationed along Sela Pass still report that the spirit of Jaswant Singh Rawat patrols the bunkers and keeps watch over the icy frontier.",
      "The Vanishing Snow Lotus: Local lamas believe that sighting the rare Brahma Kamal blooming wild in the high moraines grants profound spiritual clarity."
    ],
