    "coverImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dd/Temple_No.-_3%2C_Nalanda_Archaeological_Site.jpg/1280px-Temple_No.-_3%2C_Nalanda_Archaeological_Site.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail",
    "description": "The ruins of the world’s greatest residential university where scholars from China, Korea, Japan, and Persia studied philosophy, astronomy, and medicine for over 800 years.",
    "longDescription": "Tradition holds that Nalanda was named after the Naga serpent named Nalanda who dwelt in a sacred mango grove bought by 500 merchant traders for Lord Buddha. Another translation means \"Giver of Knowledge Without End\" (Na-alam-da). It is said that when the philosopher Nagarjuna taught here, celestial beings gathered in the courtyards to listen.\n\nFounded in the 5th century CE under the patronage of the Gupta Empire and expanded by King Harshavardhana and Pala monarchs, Nalanda housed 10,000 students and 2,000 teachers. Its multi-storied library, Dharmaganja, was so vast that when it was set ablaze during the 1193 invasion, Persian historians recorded that manuscripts burned continuously for three full months.\n\nNalanda invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Bihar.",
    "loreTitle": "The Library of Burning Wisdom & The Golden Gate of Nalanda",
    "ancientLegend": "Tradition holds that Nalanda was named after the Naga serpent named Nalanda who dwelt in a sacred mango grove bought by 500 merchant traders for Lord Buddha. Another translation means \"Giver of Knowledge Without End\" (Na-alam-da). It is said that when the philosopher Nagarjuna taught here, celestial beings gathered in the courtyards to listen.",
    "historicalChronicle": "Founded in the 5th century CE under the patronage of the Gupta Empire and expanded by King Harshavardhana and Pala monarchs, Nalanda housed 10,000 students and 2,000 teachers. Its multi-storied library, Dharmaganja, was so vast that when it was set ablaze during the 1193 invasion, Persian historians recorded that manuscripts burned continuously for three full months.",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The Pilgrim Footsteps of Xuanzang: Chinese traveler Xuanzang lived and studied here for five years, describing a complex of 300 rooms, stone water clocks, and sky-touching stupas.",
      "The Sariputra Stupa Miracle: The iconic tiered stupa dedicated to Buddha’s chief disciple is said to have hidden relic chambers that were sealed with interlocking brickwork that has baffled modern structural engineers."
    ],
