    "coverImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/Ancient_Temple%2C_Gulmarg.jpg/1280px-Ancient_Temple%2C_Gulmarg.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail",
    "description": "Perched in the Pir Panjal mountain range, Gulmarg is a legendary alpine wonderland of wildflower meadows, pine forests, and one of the highest cable cars in the world soaring to 13,780 feet.",
    "longDescription": "Originally named Gaurimarg (\"The Path of Gauri\") in honor of Goddess Parvati by Hindu ascetics, the valley was renamed Gulmarg (\"Meadow of Flowers\") in the 16th century by Sultan Yusuf Shah Chak, who was enchanted by its rolling pastures adorned with 21 varieties of wild alpine blooms.\n\nA favorite summer retreat of Mughal Emperor Jahangir, who collected over twenty species of wild mountain flora here, Gulmarg was developed as a premier winter sports center in the 1920s. The Gulmarg Gondola carries skiers and mountaineers to the shoulder of Mount Apharwat overlooking the frozen alp of Alpather Lake.\n\nGulmarg invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Jammu and Kashmir.",
    "loreTitle": "The Meadow of Flowers & The Gondola of Apharwat Peak",
    "ancientLegend": "Originally named Gaurimarg (\"The Path of Gauri\") in honor of Goddess Parvati by Hindu ascetics, the valley was renamed Gulmarg (\"Meadow of Flowers\") in the 16th century by Sultan Yusuf Shah Chak, who was enchanted by its rolling pastures adorned with 21 varieties of wild alpine blooms.",
    "historicalChronicle": "A favorite summer retreat of Mughal Emperor Jahangir, who collected over twenty species of wild mountain flora here, Gulmarg was developed as a premier winter sports center in the 1920s. The Gulmarg Gondola carries skiers and mountaineers to the shoulder of Mount Apharwat overlooking the frozen alp of Alpather Lake.",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The Frozen Lake of Apharwat: Alpather Lake remains frozen until late June; Gujjar shepherd folklore claims that throwing a single pebble into its icy waters summons sudden mountain mist and thunder.",
      "The Maharani Temple Bell: The red-roofed Shiva temple on a grassy knoll was built in 1915 by Queen Mohini Bai Sisodia, wife of Maharaja Hari Singh, and can be seen from any vantage point in the valley."
    ],
