    "coverImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Complex_of_Virupaksha_Temple%2C_Hampi_%2804%29.jpg/1280px-Complex_of_Virupaksha_Temple%2C_Hampi_%2804%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail",
    "description": "Spanning 4,000 hectares across a surreal landscape of giant balancing granite boulders along the Tungabhadra River, Hampi was the capital of the magnificent Vijayanagara Empire.",
    "longDescription": "According to the Ramayana, Hampi was the mythical monkey kingdom of Kishkindha where Sugriva, Vali, and Hanuman lived. Matanga Hill is named after Sage Matanga whose curse protected Sugriva, and Anjaneya Hill across the river is worshipped as the divine birthplace of Lord Hanuman.\n\nIn the 15th and 16th centuries under Emperor Sri Krishnadevaraya, Vijayanagara was the second largest city in the world after Beijing, trading diamonds, rubies, and Arabian war horses along wide bazaar streets. Sacked in 1565 at the Battle of Talikota, its grand monuments like the Virupaksha Temple, the Monolithic Narasimha, and the iconic Stone Chariot at Vittala Temple remain UNESCO World Heritage wonders.\n\nHampi invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Karnataka.",
    "loreTitle": "The Golden City of Krishnadevaraya & The Stone Chariot",
    "ancientLegend": "According to the Ramayana, Hampi was the mythical monkey kingdom of Kishkindha where Sugriva, Vali, and Hanuman lived. Matanga Hill is named after Sage Matanga whose curse protected Sugriva, and Anjaneya Hill across the river is worshipped as the divine birthplace of Lord Hanuman.",
    "historicalChronicle": "In the 15th and 16th centuries under Emperor Sri Krishnadevaraya, Vijayanagara was the second largest city in the world after Beijing, trading diamonds, rubies, and Arabian war horses along wide bazaar streets. Sacked in 1565 at the Battle of Talikota, its grand monuments like the Virupaksha Temple, the Monolithic Narasimha, and the iconic Stone Chariot at Vittala Temple remain UNESCO World Heritage wonders.",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The Musical Pillars of Vittala: The 56 stone pillars of the Ranga Mandapa produce musical notes (Sa-Re-Ga-Ma) and percussion beats when tapped lightly with knuckles, carved with hollow internal acoustic channels.",
      "The Market Where Diamonds Were Weighed: Portuguese chroniclers Domingo Paes and Fernão Nunes recorded with astonishment that diamonds, emeralds, and pearls were sold openly by the kilogram along the street outside Virupaksha Temple."
    ],
