    "coverImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f0/Banasurasagar_Main_Dam.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled",
    "description": "Perched high in the Western Ghats, Wayanad (\"The Land of Paddy Fields\") is a lush highland of misty rainforests, spice plantations, and prehistoric rock art dating back to 6000 BCE.",
    "longDescription": "According to regional folklore, the split rock fissure of Edakkal Caves was created when Lava and Kusha, the twin sons of Lord Rama, shot arrows into the mountain during the Ramayana. The natural cave walls contain Neolithic petroglyphs of human figures with headgear, bows, wheels, and animals carved 8,000 years ago.\n\nWayanad was the impenetrable guerrilla forest stronghold of Pazhassi Raja (the \"Lion of Kerala\"), who fought the British East India Company using indigenous Kurichiya tribal bowmen in the late 18th century. The Banasura Sagar Dam is India’s largest earth dam, cradling a chain of floating green islands.\n\nWayanad invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Kerala.",
    "loreTitle": "The Arrows of Lava-Kusha & The Prehistoric Rock Carvings",
    "ancientLegend": "According to regional folklore, the split rock fissure of Edakkal Caves was created when Lava and Kusha, the twin sons of Lord Rama, shot arrows into the mountain during the Ramayana. The natural cave walls contain Neolithic petroglyphs of human figures with headgear, bows, wheels, and animals carved 8,000 years ago.",
    "historicalChronicle": "Wayanad was the impenetrable guerrilla forest stronghold of Pazhassi Raja (the \"Lion of Kerala\"), who fought the British East India Company using indigenous Kurichiya tribal bowmen in the late 18th century. The Banasura Sagar Dam is India’s largest earth dam, cradling a chain of floating green islands.",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The Chained Tree of Lakkidi: A massive banyan tree on the Thamarassery ghat pass has a heavy steel chain tied around it; legend says the spirit of a tribal guide named Karinthandan was bound to the tree by a sorcerer after an English engineer betrayed him.",
      "The Heart-Shaped Chembra Lake: Near the summit of Chembra Peak (the highest peak in Wayanad) lies Hridaya Saras, a natural heart-shaped lake that has never dried up in living memory."
    ],
