    "coverImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/Gwalior_Fort_front.jpg/1280px-Gwalior_Fort_front.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail",
    "description": "Dominated by the colossal 8th-century Gwalior Fort perched atop an isolated sandstone hill, Gwalior is a cradle of classical Hindustani music, Scindia palaces, and monumental rock-cut Jain statues.",
    "longDescription": "The fort was founded by King Suraj Sen, who was cured of leprosy by the hermit saint Gwalipa with water from a sacred spring. In gratitude, the king named the hill citadel Gwalior. Mughal Emperor Babur famously marveled at the citadel, calling it \"the pearl among the fortresses of Hind\".\n\nThe fort features the magnificent Man Mandir Palace built by Raja Man Singh Tomar in 1508, adorned with turquoise and yellow glazed tiles depicting ducks, tigers, and plantains. It is also the resting place of musical genius Mian Tansen, one of the nine gems (Navratnas) of Emperor Akbar’s court.\n\nGwalior invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Madhya Pradesh.",
    "loreTitle": "The Unbreachable Rock Fortress & The Magic Tamarind of Tansen",
    "ancientLegend": "The fort was founded by King Suraj Sen, who was cured of leprosy by the hermit saint Gwalipa with water from a sacred spring. In gratitude, the king named the hill citadel Gwalior. Mughal Emperor Babur famously marveled at the citadel, calling it \"the pearl among the fortresses of Hind\".",
    "historicalChronicle": "The fort features the magnificent Man Mandir Palace built by Raja Man Singh Tomar in 1508, adorned with turquoise and yellow glazed tiles depicting ducks, tigers, and plantains. It is also the resting place of musical genius Mian Tansen, one of the nine gems (Navratnas) of Emperor Akbar’s court.",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The Singing of Raga Megh Malhar: Legend says when Tansen sang Raga Deepak, oil lamps lit spontaneously with heat; he then sang Raga Megh Malhar to summon cooling monsoon rainclouds from the sky.",
      "The Tamarind Tree of Sweet Voice: Vocalists from across India travel to Tansen’s tomb in Gwalior to chew leaves from the tamarind tree planted beside his grave, believing it grants a melodious singing voice."
    ],
