    "coverImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c3/Ajanta_%2863%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled",
    "description": "Carved into the sheer volcanic cliffs of a dramatic horseshoe-shaped river gorge between the 2nd century BCE and 5th century CE, Ajanta houses the zenith of ancient Buddhist painting and sculpture.",
    "longDescription": "According to monastic lore, enlightened monks chose this secluded river gorge because the natural basalt rock formations resonated with the acoustics of meditative chanting. In 1819, British officer John Smith stumbled upon Cave 10 while hunting tigers, scratching his name onto a pillar that had been hidden by wild jungle for over a millennium.\n\nThe 30 rock-cut caves served as residential viharas and prayer chaityas. Cave 1 contains the iconic masterwork mural of Bodhisattva Padmapani holding a blue lotus flower, rendered with expressive half-closed eyes and natural mineral pigments (lapis lazuli from Afghanistan, red ochre, and green malachite) that retain their luminous glow.\n\nAjanta Caves invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Maharashtra.",
    "loreTitle": "The Horseshoe Gorge of Waghur & The Murals of Padmapani",
    "ancientLegend": "According to monastic lore, enlightened monks chose this secluded river gorge because the natural basalt rock formations resonated with the acoustics of meditative chanting. In 1819, British officer John Smith stumbled upon Cave 10 while hunting tigers, scratching his name onto a pillar that had been hidden by wild jungle for over a millennium.",
    "historicalChronicle": "The 30 rock-cut caves served as residential viharas and prayer chaityas. Cave 1 contains the iconic masterwork mural of Bodhisattva Padmapani holding a blue lotus flower, rendered with expressive half-closed eyes and natural mineral pigments (lapis lazuli from Afghanistan, red ochre, and green malachite) that retain their luminous glow.",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The Light-Catching Mirrors: Ancient artists illuminated the deep, dark cave interiors by using polished brass shields and white cotton sheets placed at the cave entrance to bounce morning sunlight onto the wet plaster walls.",
      "The Reclining Parinirvana Buddha: Cave 26 contains a monumental 24-foot rock carving of the reclining Buddha entering Mahaparinirvana, with grieving disciples carved below and rejoicing celestial devas welcoming him above."
    ],
