    "coverImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Ellora_Caves%2C_India%2C_Majestic_Kailasa_Temple.jpg/1280px-Ellora_Caves%2C_India%2C_Majestic_Kailasa_Temple.jpg",
    "description": "A UNESCO World Heritage masterpiece featuring 34 monolithic rock-cut cave temples spanning Hindu, Buddhist, and Jain traditions, crowned by the breathtaking Kailasa Temple (Cave 16).",
    "longDescription": "According to the Katha-Kalpataru, Queen Manikavati of the Rashtrakuta dynasty vowed to fast until she saw the pinnacle (shikhara) of a temple dedicated to Shiva. Master architect Kokasa promised the king that the queen could break her fast within a week: he began carving the temple from the top of the mountain downwards, revealing the shikhara first before excavating the lower courtyards.\n\nThe Kailasa Temple is the largest monolithic rock excavation in human history. Over 200,000 tons of solid volcanic basalt rock were hand-chiseled away from the cliff face over a hundred years under King Krishna I (756–773 CE). The two-storied temple features colossal life-sized elephants, a 100-foot gateway, and a relief depicting demon Ravana shaking Mount Kailash.\n\nEllora Caves invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Maharashtra.",
    "loreTitle": "The Mountain Carved from the Heavens & The Chariot of Shiva",
    "ancientLegend": "According to the Katha-Kalpataru, Queen Manikavati of the Rashtrakuta dynasty vowed to fast until she saw the pinnacle (shikhara) of a temple dedicated to Shiva. Master architect Kokasa promised the king that the queen could break her fast within a week: he began carving the temple from the top of the mountain downwards, revealing the shikhara first before excavating the lower courtyards.",
    "historicalChronicle": "The Kailasa Temple is the largest monolithic rock excavation in human history. Over 200,000 tons of solid volcanic basalt rock were hand-chiseled away from the cliff face over a hundred years under King Krishna I (756–773 CE). The two-storied temple features colossal life-sized elephants, a 100-foot gateway, and a relief depicting demon Ravana shaking Mount Kailash.",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The Chisel Marks of Cave 16: Modern engineers estimate that excavating Kailasa with modern pneumatic machinery would take decades, yet ancient sculptors removed two million cubic feet of rock without a single structural miscalculation.",
      "The Indra Sabha Jain Cave: Cave 32 features an exquisitely carved double-storied Jain assembly hall with an open lotus flower on the ceiling and a monolithic stone shrine dedicated to Lord Mahavira."
    ],
