    "coverImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/Mumbai_03-2016_30_Gateway_of_India.jpg/1280px-Mumbai_03-2016_30_Gateway_of_India.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail",
    "description": "India’s financial and creative powerhouse, Mumbai evolved from seven volcanic islands inhabited by indigenous Koli fisherfolk into a dazzling metropolis of Victorian Gothic architecture and Marine Drive promenades.",
    "longDescription": "The city takes its name from Mumba Devi, the eight-armed patron goddess of the indigenous Koli fishing community who has protected seafaring fisherfolk from Arabian cyclones for millennia. Across the harbor on Elephanta Island sits the colossal 6th-century rock-cut Trimurti sculpture depicting Shiva as Creator, Preserver, and Destroyer.\n\nCeded to King Charles II of England in 1661 as part of Catherine of Braganza’s Portuguese wedding dowry, the Hornby Vellard reclamation project merged the seven separate islands into a single landmass. The Gateway of India, built to commemorate King George V’s 1911 visit, later witnessed the departure of the last British soldiers in 1948.\n\nMumbai invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Maharashtra.",
    "loreTitle": "The Seven Islands of Mumbadevi & The Gateway to the East",
    "ancientLegend": "The city takes its name from Mumba Devi, the eight-armed patron goddess of the indigenous Koli fishing community who has protected seafaring fisherfolk from Arabian cyclones for millennia. Across the harbor on Elephanta Island sits the colossal 6th-century rock-cut Trimurti sculpture depicting Shiva as Creator, Preserver, and Destroyer.",
    "historicalChronicle": "Ceded to King Charles II of England in 1661 as part of Catherine of Braganza’s Portuguese wedding dowry, the Hornby Vellard reclamation project merged the seven separate islands into a single landmass. The Gateway of India, built to commemorate King George V’s 1911 visit, later witnessed the departure of the last British soldiers in 1948.",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The Dabbawalas Six Sigma Miracle: Over 5,000 Mumbai Dabbawalas deliver 200,000 hot home-cooked lunchboxes across suburban trains daily using a color-coded marking system that achieves a world-record accuracy rate of one mistake in six million deliveries.",
      "The Haji Ali Dargah Tide Walk: The 15th-century white marble tomb of Sufi saint Haji Ali Shah Bukhari sits on an islet 500 meters into the Arabian Sea; the stone causeway is submerged during high tide, allowing pilgrims to walk to the shrine only when the sea retreats."
    ],
