    "coverImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/Shri_Jagannath_temple.jpg/1280px-Shri_Jagannath_temple.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail",
    "description": "One of the four supreme Char Dham pilgrimage shrines, Puri is the sacred kingdom of Lord Jagannath, renowned for the Rath Yatra chariot festival and the ancient golden surf of the Bay of Bengal.",
    "longDescription": "King Indradyumna was instructed in a dream to find a sacred neem log floating in the ocean. The divine architect Vishwakarma appeared as an old carpenter named Maharana, agreeing to carve the deities on the condition that no one open the temple doors for 21 days. When the impatient queen opened the door on the fourteenth day, the carpenter vanished, leaving the deities with large round compassionate eyes and unfinished limbs.\n\nThe 12th-century Jagannath Temple features a 65-meter stone spire hoisting the sacred Patitapavana flag (Neelachakra), which mysteriously always flutters against the wind direction. Every twelve to nineteen years during Nabakalebara, the wooden bodies of the deities are ritually buried and renewed from sacred neem trees.\n\nPuri invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Odisha.",
    "loreTitle": "The Mysterious Heart of Jagannath & The World’s Largest Kitchen",
    "ancientLegend": "King Indradyumna was instructed in a dream to find a sacred neem log floating in the ocean. The divine architect Vishwakarma appeared as an old carpenter named Maharana, agreeing to carve the deities on the condition that no one open the temple doors for 21 days. When the impatient queen opened the door on the fourteenth day, the carpenter vanished, leaving the deities with large round compassionate eyes and unfinished limbs.",
    "historicalChronicle": "The 12th-century Jagannath Temple features a 65-meter stone spire hoisting the sacred Patitapavana flag (Neelachakra), which mysteriously always flutters against the wind direction. Every twelve to nineteen years during Nabakalebara, the wooden bodies of the deities are ritually buried and renewed from sacred neem trees.",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The Secret \"Brahma Padartha\" in the Heart: During the midnight Nabakalebara ritual, a blindfolded head priest with cloth-wrapped hands transfers the mysterious living core (Brahma Padartha) from the old wooden idol into the new one; legend says anyone who sees it with bare eyes would perish.",
      "The Seven Clay Pots of the Temple Kitchen: In the temple’s mega-kitchen (Ananda Bazar), seven earthen pots are placed on top of each other over a single wood fire; miraculously, the food in the uppermost pot cooks first, and the food in the lowest pot cooks last."
    ],
