    "coverImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/Alwar_fort_or_Bala_Quila_09.jpg/1280px-Alwar_fort_or_Bala_Quila_09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail",
    "description": "Framed by the rugged Aravalli ridges, Alwar is a historic kingdom of Rajput-Mughal architecture, marble stepwells, the Sariska tiger reserve, and the haunted ruins of Bhangarh Fort.",
    "longDescription": "According to the Mahabharata, the ancient Matsya kingdom was centered around Viratnagar in Alwar, where the Pandavas spent their 13th year of disguised exile (Agyatvas). Sages tell of the cursed ruins of Bhangarh Fort nearby, where a dark tantric wizard cursed the entire royal town to fall into instant destruction after Princess Ratnavati rejected his love potion.\n\nFounded in 1770 by Pratap Singh, Alwar boasts the imposing 10th-century Bala Qila (\"Young Fort\") with 51 large and 446 small bastions. The City Palace houses the Moosi Maharani ki Chhatri, an exquisite double-storied red sandstone and white marble cenotaph built over the royal lake.\n\nAlwar invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Rajasthan.",
    "loreTitle": "The Bala Qila Lookout & The Curse of the Bhangarh Sorcerer",
    "ancientLegend": "According to the Mahabharata, the ancient Matsya kingdom was centered around Viratnagar in Alwar, where the Pandavas spent their 13th year of disguised exile (Agyatvas). Sages tell of the cursed ruins of Bhangarh Fort nearby, where a dark tantric wizard cursed the entire royal town to fall into instant destruction after Princess Ratnavati rejected his love potion.",
    "historicalChronicle": "Founded in 1770 by Pratap Singh, Alwar boasts the imposing 10th-century Bala Qila (\"Young Fort\") with 51 large and 446 small bastions. The City Palace houses the Moosi Maharani ki Chhatri, an exquisite double-storied red sandstone and white marble cenotaph built over the royal lake.",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The Midnight Law of Bhangarh: The Archaeological Survey of India has a formal signboard strictly prohibiting anyone from entering the ruins of Bhangarh Fort between sunset and sunrise due to unexplained paranormal occurrences.",
      "The Famous Alwar Kalakand: In 1947, sweet-maker Baba Thakur Das created the original soft, caramelized milk cake (Alwar Ka Mawa/Kalakand), now a culinary legend across Northern India."
    ],
