    "coverImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Garh_Palace_and_Taragarh_Fort%2C_Bundi_2011-12-26_EK_II.jpg/1280px-Garh_Palace_and_Taragarh_Fort%2C_Bundi_2011-12-26_EK_II.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail",
    "description": "A picturesque medieval town nestled in a narrow Aravalli gorge, Bundi is famous for its intricate stepwells (Baoris), the majestic Taragarh Fort, and the exquisite miniature frescoes of Chitrashala.",
    "longDescription": "Bundi was named after the Meena chieftain Bunda Singh who ruled the valley until the Hada Rajput kings established their dynasty in 1342. Rudyard Kipling stayed at Bundi’s Sukh Mahal and wrote that the towering Taragarh Palace seemed \"the work of goblins rather than of men\".\n\nBundi is the stepwell capital of India, with over 50 royal baoris. The 1699 Raniji ki Baori (Queen’s Stepwell) descends 46 meters with 100 stone steps carved with statues of the incarnations of Vishnu. The Garh Palace houses the Chitrashala gallery, adorned with vibrant turquoise, indigo, and gold frescoes of Radha-Krishna.\n\nBundi invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Rajasthan.",
    "loreTitle": "The Turquoise Murals of Chitrashala & The 84-Pillared Cenotaph",
    "ancientLegend": "Bundi was named after the Meena chieftain Bunda Singh who ruled the valley until the Hada Rajput kings established their dynasty in 1342. Rudyard Kipling stayed at Bundi’s Sukh Mahal and wrote that the towering Taragarh Palace seemed \"the work of goblins rather than of men\".",
    "historicalChronicle": "Bundi is the stepwell capital of India, with over 50 royal baoris. The 1699 Raniji ki Baori (Queen’s Stepwell) descends 46 meters with 100 stone steps carved with statues of the incarnations of Vishnu. The Garh Palace houses the Chitrashala gallery, adorned with vibrant turquoise, indigo, and gold frescoes of Radha-Krishna.",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The 84-Pillared Cenotaph (Chaurasi Khambon ki Chhatri): Built in 1683 by Rao Raja Anirudh Singh to honor his wet-nurse Deva, the two-storied cenotaph is supported by 84 carved stone pillars that are said to be impossible to count correctly twice.",
      "The Dragon Cannon of Taragarh: The giant 16th-century bronze cannon \"Garbha Gunjan\" (Thunder from the Womb) atop Taragarh Fort was so deafening that its shockwave was said to cause expecting mothers to swoon for miles around."
    ],
