    "coverImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/81/Delwada.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled",
    "description": "Rajasthan’s only hill station, perched at 4,000 feet in the Aravallis, Mount Abu is famous for the Nakki Lake, the sacred sunset peaks, and the world’s most exquisite carved Jain temples at Dilwara.",
    "longDescription": "According to the Bhavishya Purana, Sage Vashistha performed a grand fire sacrifice (Agnikunda) atop Mount Abu to create the four legendary Agnikula Rajput warrior clans (Pratihara, Paramara, Chauhans, and Solankis) to protect righteousness on earth. Nakki Lake is said to have been carved out of the rocky mountain by gods using their bare fingernails (Nakh).\n\nThe Dilwara Temples, built between the 11th and 13th centuries by ministers Vimal Shah and Vastupala-Tejpala, feature white marble carvings so translucent that the stone resembles delicate carved sugar icing. Craftsmen were reportedly paid with gold dust equal to the weight of marble dust they chiseled away.\n\nMount Abu invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Rajasthan.",
    "loreTitle": "The Agnikula Fire Pit of Sage Vashistha & The Translucent Marble of Dilwara",
    "ancientLegend": "According to the Bhavishya Purana, Sage Vashistha performed a grand fire sacrifice (Agnikunda) atop Mount Abu to create the four legendary Agnikula Rajput warrior clans (Pratihara, Paramara, Chauhans, and Solankis) to protect righteousness on earth. Nakki Lake is said to have been carved out of the rocky mountain by gods using their bare fingernails (Nakh).",
    "historicalChronicle": "The Dilwara Temples, built between the 11th and 13th centuries by ministers Vimal Shah and Vastupala-Tejpala, feature white marble carvings so translucent that the stone resembles delicate carved sugar icing. Craftsmen were reportedly paid with gold dust equal to the weight of marble dust they chiseled away.",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The Toad Rock Lookout: Overlooking Nakki Lake rests a massive natural rock formation sculpted by wind erosion to resemble a giant toad about to leap into the water.",
      "The Gurushikhar Bell at 5,650 Feet: Gurushikhar is the highest peak in the Aravalli Range; at its summit sits an ancient temple of Dattatreya whose giant bronze bell resonates across the cloud valleys when struck."
    ],
