    "coverImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5f/Gurudongmar_Lake_Sikkim%2C_India_%28edit%29.jpg/1280px-Gurudongmar_Lake_Sikkim%2C_India_%28edit%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail",
    "description": "A peaceful alpine village at 8,838 feet in North Sikkim, Lachen (\"Big Pass\") is the traditional home of the indigenous Lachenpas and the gateway to the sacred, frozen Gurudongmar Lake.",
    "longDescription": "Lachen is the starting point for journeys to Gurudongmar Lake (17,800 ft), one of the highest lakes in the world. According to legend, when local nomads complained that the lake froze completely in winter leaving no drinking water, Guru Padmasambhava (Guru Ninpoche) touched a portion of the lake; that specific spot has miraculously never frozen solid even in minus-35-degree blizzards.\n\nThe village operates under the unique Dzumsa—an ancient democratic self-governing tribal council system where a village headman (Pipon) is elected annually to manage pastures, yak migrations, and timber harvesting with absolute community harmony.\n\nLachen invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Sikkim.",
    "loreTitle": "The Valley of Big Mountain Passes & The Living Lachenpas",
    "ancientLegend": "Lachen is the starting point for journeys to Gurudongmar Lake (17,800 ft), one of the highest lakes in the world. According to legend, when local nomads complained that the lake froze completely in winter leaving no drinking water, Guru Padmasambhava (Guru Ninpoche) touched a portion of the lake; that specific spot has miraculously never frozen solid even in minus-35-degree blizzards.",
    "historicalChronicle": "The village operates under the unique Dzumsa—an ancient democratic self-governing tribal council system where a village headman (Pipon) is elected annually to manage pastures, yak migrations, and timber harvesting with absolute community harmony.",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The Blessing of Guru Nanak at Gurudongmar: Sikh traditions also recount that Guru Nanak visited the high lake during his travels to Tibet, striking the ice with his walking stick to provide fresh water to nomads.",
      "The Yak Wool Blankets: Lachenpa women spin thick hand-woven sheep and yak wool rugs and blankets on traditional wooden looms, dyed using local mountain moss and walnut bark."
    ],
