    "coverImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d7/Yumthang_valley%2C_Lachung_Sikkim_India_2012.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled",
    "description": "Nestled at 8,610 feet against sheer snow peaks in North Sikkim, Lachung (\"Small Pass\") is an idyllic mountain valley renowned for the Yumthang Valley of Flowers, hot sulphur springs, and apple orchards.",
    "longDescription": "Sikkimese mountain lore speaks of Yumthang Valley as the summer grazing playground of celestial spirits who carpet the alpine meadows with forty species of wild rhododendrons, primulas, and blue poppies during the spring thaw.\n\nLike Lachen, Lachung preserves the traditional Dzumsa administrative council. The 19th-century Lachung Monastery sits amidst apple and peach orchards on the opposite bank of the roaring Lachung Chu river, featuring colorful murals of the Tibetan wheel of life.\n\nLachung invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Sikkim.",
    "loreTitle": "The Shingba Rhododendron Sanctuary & The Valley of Flowers of the North",
    "ancientLegend": "Sikkimese mountain lore speaks of Yumthang Valley as the summer grazing playground of celestial spirits who carpet the alpine meadows with forty species of wild rhododendrons, primulas, and blue poppies during the spring thaw.",
    "historicalChronicle": "Like Lachen, Lachung preserves the traditional Dzumsa administrative council. The 19th-century Lachung Monastery sits amidst apple and peach orchards on the opposite bank of the roaring Lachung Chu river, featuring colorful murals of the Tibetan wheel of life.",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The Healing Waters of Yumthang: A small wooden bridge across the river leads to natural hot sulphur springs where travelers and yak herders bathe in thermal waters rich in therapeutic minerals.",
      "The Zero Point Snow Frontier: Beyond Yumthang lies Yumesamdong (Zero Point at 15,300 ft), where three mountain rivers converge amidst perpetual snowfields at the frontier of the high Tibetan plateau."
    ],
