    "coverImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/An_aerial_view_of_Madurai_city_from_atop_of_Meenakshi_Amman_temple.jpg/1280px-An_aerial_view_of_Madurai_city_from_atop_of_Meenakshi_Amman_temple.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail",
    "description": "Continuously inhabited for over 2,500 years on the Vaigai River, Madurai is the soul of Tamil culture, dominated by the magnificent Meenakshi Amman Temple with fourteen soaring multi-colored gopurams.",
    "longDescription": "Queen Meenakshi was born from a holy sacrificial fire with three breasts and eyes shaped like fish (Meenakshi). Prophecy foretold that her third breast would vanish when she met her future husband. She conquered all four corners of the world, but when she arrived at Mount Kailash to fight Lord Shiva, she locked eyes with him, her third breast vanished, and she married him as Sundareswarar.\n\nMadurai was the historic seat of the three ancient Tamil Sangam literary assemblies. The Meenakshi Temple, restored to its grandeur by King Thirumalai Nayak in the 17th century, contains the Hall of a Thousand Pillars (Ayiram Kaal Mandapam) carved with mythical Yali beasts, and musical stone pillars that ring like bells.\n\nMadurai invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Tamil Nadu.",
    "loreTitle": "The Three-Breasted Warrior Queen & The Thousand Pillars of Meenakshi",
    "ancientLegend": "Queen Meenakshi was born from a holy sacrificial fire with three breasts and eyes shaped like fish (Meenakshi). Prophecy foretold that her third breast would vanish when she met her future husband. She conquered all four corners of the world, but when she arrived at Mount Kailash to fight Lord Shiva, she locked eyes with him, her third breast vanished, and she married him as Sundareswarar.",
    "historicalChronicle": "Madurai was the historic seat of the three ancient Tamil Sangam literary assemblies. The Meenakshi Temple, restored to its grandeur by King Thirumalai Nayak in the 17th century, contains the Hall of a Thousand Pillars (Ayiram Kaal Mandapam) carved with mythical Yali beasts, and musical stone pillars that ring like bells.",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The Jasmine Flower of Madurai (Madurai Malli): Madurai’s GI-tagged jasmine has a distinct thick petal structure and intoxicating scent that lasts for days, traditionally worn in the hair of women throughout Tamil Nadu.",
      "The Chithirai Festival Wedding: Every April, over one million devotees flood the streets of Madurai to celebrate the celestial wedding (Thirukalyanam) of Goddess Meenakshi and Lord Sundareswarar with colossal wooden chariots."
    ],
