    "coverImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dd/Brihadisvara_Temple_during_Maha_Shivaratri-WUS03611_%28edit%29.jpg/1280px-Brihadisvara_Temple_during_Maha_Shivaratri-WUS03611_%28edit%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail",
    "description": "The imperial capital of the Chola Empire, Thanjavur is the home of the UNESCO World Heritage Brihadisvara Temple (Big Temple), Tanjore painting on gold foil, and bronze casting mastery.",
    "longDescription": "Built by the great Emperor Raja Raja Chola I between 1003 and 1010 CE, the Brihadisvara Temple stands as one of humanity’s greatest architectural achievements. The 216-foot granite vimana (temple tower) is crowned by an 81-ton monolithic stone cupola (Kumbam), raised to the summit along a 6-kilometer earthen ramp built by thousands of war elephants.\n\nThe temple was constructed entirely of interlocking granite stones without any mortar or binding cement. Thanjavur is also the birthplace of classic Tanjore Paintings—richly colored artworks crafted with 22-karat gold foil leaf, semi-precious stones, and limestone relief on wooden planks.\n\nThanjavur invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Tamil Nadu.",
    "loreTitle": "The Grand Vimana of Raja Raja Chola & The Eighty-Ton Capstone",
    "ancientLegend": "Built by the great Emperor Raja Raja Chola I between 1003 and 1010 CE, the Brihadisvara Temple stands as one of humanity’s greatest architectural achievements. The 216-foot granite vimana (temple tower) is crowned by an 81-ton monolithic stone cupola (Kumbam), raised to the summit along a 6-kilometer earthen ramp built by thousands of war elephants.",
    "historicalChronicle": "The temple was constructed entirely of interlocking granite stones without any mortar or binding cement. Thanjavur is also the birthplace of classic Tanjore Paintings—richly colored artworks crafted with 22-karat gold foil leaf, semi-precious stones, and limestone relief on wooden planks.",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The Shadowless Vimana Mystery: The architectural alignment of the massive vimana pyramid is so precise that during the summer equinox, the shadow of the central tower does not fall outside the temple courtyard.",
      "The 13-Ton Monolithic Nandi: The colossal stone Nandi bull at the temple entrance was carved from a single block of granite; folklore says the stone was growing continuously until priests inserted a brass nail into its back."
    ],
