    "coverImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/1000pillar_temple_warangal.jpg/1280px-1000pillar_temple_warangal.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail",
    "description": "The ancient capital of the Kakatiya dynasty, Warangal is famous for the Thousand Pillar Temple, the massive Warangal Fort stone gateways (Kirti Toranas), and the UNESCO World Heritage Ramappa Temple.",
    "longDescription": "Warangal was anciently known as Orugallu (\"Single Stone\"), named after a massive single boulder outcropping around which King Prola II built his fortress. Kakatiya rulers like the legendary warrior queen Rani Rudrama Devi ruled the Deccan with progressive irrigation tanks and unmatched stone masonry.\n\nThe Ramappa Temple (Rudreswara), built in 1213 CE by General Recharla Rudra, was praised by Marco Polo as \"the brightest star in the galaxy of medieval temples\". It was constructed using lightweight \"floating bricks\" that are so porous they float on water, protecting the structure from earthquake damage for 800 years.\n\nWarangal invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Telangana.",
    "loreTitle": "The Star Platform of Hanamkonda & The Floating Bricks of Ramappa",
    "ancientLegend": "Warangal was anciently known as Orugallu (\"Single Stone\"), named after a massive single boulder outcropping around which King Prola II built his fortress. Kakatiya rulers like the legendary warrior queen Rani Rudrama Devi ruled the Deccan with progressive irrigation tanks and unmatched stone masonry.",
    "historicalChronicle": "The Ramappa Temple (Rudreswara), built in 1213 CE by General Recharla Rudra, was praised by Marco Polo as \"the brightest star in the galaxy of medieval temples\". It was constructed using lightweight \"floating bricks\" that are so porous they float on water, protecting the structure from earthquake damage for 800 years.",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The Dancing Bracket Sculptures of Ramappa: The black basalt bracket figures (Madanikas) supporting the temple roof are polished to a mirror finish, depicting slender celestial dancers in dynamic tribhanga postures.",
      "The Thousand Pillar Temple (Veyyi Sthambala Gudi): Built in 1163 CE in the shape of a star on Hanamkonda hill, the pillars are carved so closely that no matter where you stand, your view of the central deities is never completely blocked."
    ],
