    "coverImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/Taj_Mahal_%28Edited%29.jpeg/1280px-Taj_Mahal_%28Edited%29.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail",
    "description": "The glorious Mughal capital on the Yamuna River, Agra is world-famous for the Taj Mahal—a UNESCO World Heritage wonder of the world—the sprawling Agra Fort, and sweet white Petha.",
    "longDescription": "Mughal Emperor Shah Jahan commissioned the Taj Mahal in 1631 as a mausoleum for his beloved Empress Mumtaz Mahal, who died giving birth to their fourteenth child. Poet Rabindranath Tagore immortalized it as \"a solitary tear-drop on the cheek of time\".\n\nConstructed by 20,000 artisans over twenty-two years using translucent white Makrana marble inlaid with twenty-eight types of precious stones (Pietra Dura or Parchin Kari), the monument exhibits perfect bilateral symmetry. Agra Fort, built by Emperor Akbar in red sandstone, contains the Musamman Burj where Shah Jahan was imprisoned by his son Aurangzeb, gazing at the Taj Mahal until his death.\n\nAgra invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Uttar Pradesh.",
    "loreTitle": "The Poem in Marble of Shah Jahan & The Red Fort of Akbar",
    "ancientLegend": "Mughal Emperor Shah Jahan commissioned the Taj Mahal in 1631 as a mausoleum for his beloved Empress Mumtaz Mahal, who died giving birth to their fourteenth child. Poet Rabindranath Tagore immortalized it as \"a solitary tear-drop on the cheek of time\".",
    "historicalChronicle": "Constructed by 20,000 artisans over twenty-two years using translucent white Makrana marble inlaid with twenty-eight types of precious stones (Pietra Dura or Parchin Kari), the monument exhibits perfect bilateral symmetry. Agra Fort, built by Emperor Akbar in red sandstone, contains the Musamman Burj where Shah Jahan was imprisoned by his son Aurangzeb, gazing at the Taj Mahal until his death.",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The Black Marble Taj Myth: European traveler Jean-Baptiste Tavernier wrote of a rumored twin black marble mausoleum Shah Jahan intended to construct across the Yamuna River at Mehtab Bagh for himself.",
      "The Color-Shifting Marble: The white Makrana marble reflects changing daylight—glowing soft pink at dawn, dazzling white at midday, and radiant silver-blue beneath full moon starlight."
    ],
