    "coverImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Shri_Ram_Janambhoomi_Mandir%2C_Ayodhya_Dham.jpg/1280px-Shri_Ram_Janambhoomi_Mandir%2C_Ayodhya_Dham.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail",
    "description": "One of the seven sacred Moksha-giving cities of India, Ayodhya is the revered birthplace of Lord Rama, celebrated for the grand Ram Janmabhoomi Mandir, Hanuman Garhi, and the serene Sarayu riverfront.",
    "longDescription": "According to the Ramayana, Ayodhya (\"The Unconquerable City\") was founded by Manu, the progenitor of humanity, and served as the capital of the solar Ikshvaku dynasty. It was from the banks of the sacred Sarayu River that Lord Rama ruled with complete justice during the golden age of Ramrajya.\n\nHanuman Garhi, a 10th-century fortress temple perched on a central hill with 76 steep steps, is where Lord Hanuman is believed to have resided as the guardian protector of Ayodhya. The newly consecrated Ram Janmabhoomi Mandir in traditional Nagara style is constructed from pink Bansi Paharpur sandstone without any structural iron.\n\nAyodhya invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Uttar Pradesh.",
    "loreTitle": "The City of the Sun Dynasty & The Saryu Aarti",
    "ancientLegend": "According to the Ramayana, Ayodhya (\"The Unconquerable City\") was founded by Manu, the progenitor of humanity, and served as the capital of the solar Ikshvaku dynasty. It was from the banks of the sacred Sarayu River that Lord Rama ruled with complete justice during the golden age of Ramrajya.",
    "historicalChronicle": "Hanuman Garhi, a 10th-century fortress temple perched on a central hill with 76 steep steps, is where Lord Hanuman is believed to have resided as the guardian protector of Ayodhya. The newly consecrated Ram Janmabhoomi Mandir in traditional Nagara style is constructed from pink Bansi Paharpur sandstone without any structural iron.",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The Sarayu Dip at Guptar Ghat: Guptar Ghat is the sacred riverbank where Lord Rama completed his earthly incarnation and entered the Sarayu waters (Jal Samadhi) to return to his cosmic form as Lord Vishnu.",
      "The 2.2 Million Deepotsav Diya Record: During Diwali, the ghats of Ram Ki Paidi are illuminated with over two million earthen oil lamps, recreating the historic night when citizens welcomed Lord Rama back from fourteen years of exile."
    ],
