    "coverImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Mathura_Temple-Mathura-India0002.JPG/1280px-Mathura_Temple-Mathura-India0002.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail",
    "description": "One of the seven sacred Moksha-giving cities of India, Mathura is the holy birthplace of Lord Krishna, famous for the Krishna Janmasthan Temple, 25 ancient Yamuna ghats, and rich Mathura Peda sweets.",
    "longDescription": "According to the Bhagavata Purana, Lord Krishna was born at midnight inside the dark underground dungeon of tyrannical King Kamsa in Mathura. The infant’s divine father Vasudeva carried him across the raging Yamuna River in a wicker basket, shielded by the multi-headed serpent Sheshanaga, to the safety of Gokul.\n\nMathura was a major political and artistic capital of the Kushan Empire in the 1st–3rd centuries CE, giving birth to the famous \"Mathura School of Art\" which created the earliest stone sculptures of the Buddha and Hindu deities out of red spotted sandstone. Vishram Ghat is the sacred riverbank where Krishna rested after slaying Kamsa.\n\nMathura invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Uttar Pradesh.",
    "loreTitle": "The Prison Cell of King Kamsa & The Vishram Ghat of Lord Krishna",
    "ancientLegend": "According to the Bhagavata Purana, Lord Krishna was born at midnight inside the dark underground dungeon of tyrannical King Kamsa in Mathura. The infant’s divine father Vasudeva carried him across the raging Yamuna River in a wicker basket, shielded by the multi-headed serpent Sheshanaga, to the safety of Gokul.",
    "historicalChronicle": "Mathura was a major political and artistic capital of the Kushan Empire in the 1st–3rd centuries CE, giving birth to the famous \"Mathura School of Art\" which created the earliest stone sculptures of the Buddha and Hindu deities out of red spotted sandstone. Vishram Ghat is the sacred riverbank where Krishna rested after slaying Kamsa.",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The Peda Sweet of the Temple: The famous caramelized brown Mathura Peda is prepared by boiling pure cow’s milk for hours with cardamom and sugar, offered as the primary prasad to the deity.",
      "The Lathmar Holi of Barsana: Nearby in Barsana and Nandgaon, the traditional Lathmar Holi is celebrated where women playfully beat men shield-bearers with wooden sticks in recreation of Krishna and Radha’s teasing banter."
    ],
