    "coverImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8e/PremMandirSideViewFromCanteen.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled",
    "description": "Located in the holy Braj region, Vrindavan (\"The Forest of Tulsi\") is the mystical village where Lord Krishna spent his playful childhood, home to over 5,000 temples and sacred groves.",
    "longDescription": "The entire sacred forest was anciently carpeted with fragrant basil trees (Vrinda). Here Lord Krishna played his divine flute (Murlidhar), captivating all cows, peacocks, and gopis, and performed the celestial Maha Raas Leela on the autumn full moon night of Sharad Purnima.\n\nRediscovered in the 16th century by the saint Chaitanya Mahaprabhu and the Six Goswamis of Vrindavan, the town preserves medieval red sandstone wonders like the 1590 Govind Dev Temple built by Raja Man Singh of Amber, and the Banke Bihari Temple where the deity is revealed behind curtains in brief glimpses (Jhalak Darshan).\n\nVrindavan invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Uttar Pradesh.",
    "loreTitle": "The Enchanted Groves of Nidhivan & The 5,000 Temples of Krishna",
    "ancientLegend": "The entire sacred forest was anciently carpeted with fragrant basil trees (Vrinda). Here Lord Krishna played his divine flute (Murlidhar), captivating all cows, peacocks, and gopis, and performed the celestial Maha Raas Leela on the autumn full moon night of Sharad Purnima.",
    "historicalChronicle": "Rediscovered in the 16th century by the saint Chaitanya Mahaprabhu and the Six Goswamis of Vrindavan, the town preserves medieval red sandstone wonders like the 1590 Govind Dev Temple built by Raja Man Singh of Amber, and the Banke Bihari Temple where the deity is revealed behind curtains in brief glimpses (Jhalak Darshan).",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The Midnight Mystery of Nidhivan: Inside the dense Nidhivan grove, twisted holy basil trees stand in pairs; local folklore holds that Lord Krishna and Radha descend every midnight to perform the Raas Leela, and all gates are locked at sunset as no human is permitted to witness the divine dance.",
      "The Swaying Black Murti of Banke Bihari: Legend says the idol of Banke Bihari manifested from the deep musical meditation of saint-musician Swami Haridas in the 16th century."
    ],
