    "description": "One of the seven sacred Moksha-giving cities of India, Haridwar (\"Gateway to the Gods\") is where the emerald Ganges leaves the Himalayas, famous for the evening Ganga Aarti at Har Ki Pauri and the Kumbh Mela.",
    "longDescription": "During the cosmic churning of the ocean (Samudra Manthan), the celestial bird Garuda carried the pot of nectar (Amrit). Four drops fell to earth, and one drop splashed at the Brahma Kund at Har Ki Pauri (\"The Footstep of Hari\"), where a footprint of Lord Vishnu is imprinted into the stone wall of the riverbank.\n\nHaridwar is the spiritual entry point for pilgrims journeying to the Char Dham peaks of Uttarakhand. High atop Bilwa and Neel Parvat hills stand the twin guardian temples of Mansa Devi and Chandi Devi, reached by cable cars soaring above the river channels.\n\nHaridwar invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Uttarakhand.",
    "loreTitle": "The Footprint of Vishnu at Har Ki Pauri & The Floating Leaf Lamps",
    "ancientLegend": "During the cosmic churning of the ocean (Samudra Manthan), the celestial bird Garuda carried the pot of nectar (Amrit). Four drops fell to earth, and one drop splashed at the Brahma Kund at Har Ki Pauri (\"The Footstep of Hari\"), where a footprint of Lord Vishnu is imprinted into the stone wall of the riverbank.",
    "historicalChronicle": "Haridwar is the spiritual entry point for pilgrims journeying to the Char Dham peaks of Uttarakhand. High atop Bilwa and Neel Parvat hills stand the twin guardian temples of Mansa Devi and Chandi Devi, reached by cable cars soaring above the river channels.",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The Thousands of Floating Leaf Diyas: Every evening during the grand Ganga Aarti at Har Ki Pauri, thousands of floating leaf boats (Diyas) carrying marigold flowers and burning camphor are released onto the swift river currents, turning the Ganges into a flowing river of golden fire.",
      "The Century-Old Genealogical Bahi Records: At the ghats, hereditary family priests (Pandas) maintain handwritten genealogical parchment scrolls dating back over twenty generations, recording the family trees of millions of Indian lineages."
    ],
