    "coverImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/78/Bengal-Tiger_Corbett_Uttarakhand_Dec-2013.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled",
    "description": "Established in 1936 as India’s first national park, Jim Corbett National Park in the Himalayan foothills is celebrated for dense sal forests, the sparkling Ramganga River, and Royal Bengal tigers.",
    "longDescription": "Local Kumaoni folklore speaks of the deep Ramganga river valley as the hunting grounds of forest guardian spirits (Chamu Devta) who protected wild animals and peaceful forest dwellers from catastrophic floods and wilderness fires.\n\nOriginally named Hailey National Park, it was renamed in 1957 to honor the legendary hunter-turned-conservationist Jim Corbett, who tracked notorious man-eating tigers in the Kumaon hills and pioneered the movement to preserve India’s wild wildlife heritage.\n\nJim Corbett invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Uttarakhand.",
    "loreTitle": "The Legacy of Jim Corbett & The Roar of the Royal Bengal Tiger",
    "ancientLegend": "Local Kumaoni folklore speaks of the deep Ramganga river valley as the hunting grounds of forest guardian spirits (Chamu Devta) who protected wild animals and peaceful forest dwellers from catastrophic floods and wilderness fires.",
    "historicalChronicle": "Originally named Hailey National Park, it was renamed in 1957 to honor the legendary hunter-turned-conservationist Jim Corbett, who tracked notorious man-eating tigers in the Kumaon hills and pioneered the movement to preserve India’s wild wildlife heritage.",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The Dhikala Forest Rest House: The historic 1890s Dhikala complex sits deep inside the core reserve overlooking the vast Ramganga reservoir (Patli Dun), where wild herds of Asiatic elephants swim across the river at sunset.",
      "The Garjia Devi Rock Temple: A picturesque temple perched atop a massive isolated boulder in the middle of the Kosi River, which miraculously survived massive river floods for centuries."
    ],
